[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Dynamic Tests in JUnit 5: Using the Factory Method Pattern"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"JUnit 5 introduced the concept of dynamic tests, which are generated at runtime by a factory method annotated with @TestFactory. Unlike standard @Test methods, which are completely static and declared at compile-time, dynamic tests provide immense flexibility for data-driven testing and scenarios where the test cases themselves depend on dynamic runtime states."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding @TestFactory and DynamicTest"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A @TestFactory method is not a traditional test case. Instead, it is a factory that returns a single DynamicNode, a collection of DynamicNodes, a Stream, a Collection, an Iterable, or an Iterator of DynamicNode instances. The two primary implementations of DynamicNode are DynamicTest and DynamicContainer."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@TestFactory methods must not be private or static."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A DynamicTest is simply a pairing of a display name and an Executable (a functional interface similar to Runnable)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lifecycle callbacks like @BeforeEach and @AfterEach are NOT executed for individual dynamic tests; they only apply to the @TestFactory method itself."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Practical Code Example"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following example demonstrates how to use the Factory Method pattern to generate multiple dynamic tests using Java Streams."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.DynamicTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.TestFactory;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Stream;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.DynamicTest.dynamicTest;

public class CalculatorDynamicTest {

    private final Calculator calculator = new Calculator();

    @TestFactory
    Stream<DynamicTest> testAddition() {
        List<TestCase> testCases = Arrays.asList(
            new TestCase(1, 1, 2),
            new TestCase(2, 3, 5),
            new TestCase(10, 20, 30)
        );

        return testCases.stream().map(testCase -> 
            dynamicTest("Test sum of " + testCase.a + " and " + testCase.b, () -> {
                int result = calculator.add(testCase.a, testCase.b);
                assertEquals(testCase.expected, result);
            })
        );
    }

    private static class TestCase {
        int a;
        int b;
        int expected;

        TestCase(int a, int b, int expected) {
            this.a = a;
            this.b = b;
            this.expected = expected;
        }
    }

    private static class Calculator {
        public int add(int a, int b) {
            return a + b;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Use Cases"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dynamic tests shine when you are testing algorithms with large, programmatic input spaces, parsing configuration files to generate test assertions, or integrating with external systems where test definitions are fetched at runtime. Always ensure that the display names of your dynamic tests are unique and descriptive to make debugging easier when a failure occurs."}]
